 ## Visual Evidence Discipline
 - Read the document carefully before answering.
-- Treat the smallest exact text span as the smallest self-contained answer unit, not the shortest keyword. When the question asks for a named entry or label (for example, a business segment, product line, course or program offering, event, or comparable item), return the complete printed label of the matched entry, including conjoined qualifiers and category or product wording that belong to that entry; do not truncate it to a head noun or one component merely because the question supplies the broader field, and select the label at the category level requested rather than a narrower component label from within that entry. For comparative questions about which labeled row or segment is higher or lower, identify the selected winning cell and return the complete label attached to that same row. Include a short schedule or section designator only when it is printed as part of that same selected entry and distinguishes the requested offering; do not append a separate row label, column heading, field label, or the question's locating wording. Keep the existing brand/company/service/product shortening rule limited to questions that ask which brand, company, service, or product is advertised or named; do not apply it to a requested segment or row label. Preserve successful full multiword names and designations. This complete-label rule applies when the question asks for the named entry or label itself, rather than for an attribute value.
-- When several nearby strings look similar, use the surrounding labels or layout to identify the entry that best matches the question.
-- For questions asking for a value tied to a named table column, list row, grid coordinate, or contact field, first match the exact identifier and field label from the question to the same row/entry and contact block, then read the requested value.
-- If similar values are nearby, reject a value from an adjacent row, a different contact field (for example, telephone versus fax), or a secondary office or person block unless the question asks for that secondary entry.
-- Return only the verified value, without an explanation.
+- For questions using 'when', 'what date', or 'date and time', identify the named event and read the full printed temporal expression attached to that event. Return the complete printed date, plus the time only when the question requests a time, rather than shortening a full date to a year or substituting a year from a nearby heading, footer, or different event. If the question explicitly asks only for a year, or the named event's printed value is only a year, return that year. Preserve the printed wording and punctuation of the selected temporal value. Keep this separate from the page-number rule and from counts, names, and codes.
+- Before finalizing, identify what the question asks for: an amount, name, title, count, code, number, list, and so on. Return only that value, not the table/figure/column/row label used to locate it and not an unsolicited equivalence, conversion, alternative representation, explanation, or parenthetical note.
+- Use identifiers in the question, such as a table number, building number, study number, or record label, to locate the answer. Do not repeat them in the final answer unless the question explicitly asks for the identifier or the identifier is itself part of the requested title/name.
+- Preserve text that is genuinely part of the requested value: official names and embedded abbreviations, required units, necessary qualifiers, and all items when the question requests a plural or list answer.
+- When the document presents both a container label and its content, or both a requested value and an adjacent converted/equivalent value, prefer the content/value alone. Apply the same scope discipline when a title, name, or amount is embedded in a larger heading or cell.
+- When the question asks for a page number, page no., or the page number of a named entry, return the numeric page value alone and omit the label word "Page" and any total-page wording such as "of N", unless the question explicitly asks for the full pagination statement or the page identifier itself is a nonnumeric code. For this label portion, this page-scope rule takes priority over the general preference to copy an exact span; preserve the digits and any genuinely requested page identifier. Do not extend this to questions requesting dates, names, counts, page ranges, or full printed pagination phrases, and do not add narration or alternative values. Existing correct direct extractions and full-phrase cases explicitly requested by the question remain unchanged.
+- When the question names a record/entity and a field, first identify the exact row or entity label and the exact column or field label the question asks for. Read the value at their intersection or beside the matching field label; cross-check that the candidate's adjacent label or header matches the question, not merely a similar nearby label.
+- For contact information, distinguish exact field labels such as fax, telephone, toll-free, e-mail, and address, and return only the value attached to the requested label. Do not return a different number or code merely because it is nearby or has the same format.
+- If two candidates look plausible, prefer the one whose exact row/entity and column/field labels match the question. If none matches exactly, return the best-supported single value rather than switching to a different field or listing alternatives.
+- This procedure is especially applicable when the question includes a specific record identifier or when the page appears to contain several codes or numbers of the same format. It should not interfere with simple, directly stated single-value questions, and row/column narration must not be added to the final answer. Existing correct direct extractions remain unchanged.
 
 ## Exact Answer Discipline
 - Copy names, numbers, and dates exactly from the document whenever possible.
+- For questions requesting an amount, price, cost, or other monetary value, return the numeric value as printed in the selected cell or field, retaining any visibly attached currency symbol or unit marker as described below, except when the question explicitly states the currency or unit.
+- When the question explicitly states the currency or unit (for example, 'in $', 'in USD', or 'in dollars'), return the printed numeric amount alone and do not add or repeat that currency symbol or unit; keep the printed digits, decimal point, grouping commas, and displayed decimal places. If the question does not itself specify the currency and the selected value has a visibly attached currency symbol, preserve that symbol as currently instructed; if the question explicitly asks for the printed symbol or the full printed amount, follow that request. Do not add a currency symbol or unit that is not visibly attached.
+- Add a currency symbol or other unit marker only when that marker is visibly attached to the selected value and the question does not already state that currency or unit; do not infer or add it solely because the question asks for an amount or because the document contains other currency signs.
+- If the selected value is visibly printed with a symbol, preserve that symbol and its printed spacing exactly unless the question explicitly states the currency or unit or explicitly asks for the printed symbol or full printed amount; do not add a second representation, conversion, or parenthetical alternative.
+- This amount-specific rule, including the explicit-question exception, is scoped to monetary values and does not authorize stripping percent signs or units in other contexts, or otherwise altering dates, names, codes, or other exact values. Counts, percentages, dates, names, and codes remain governed by the existing exact-copy rules, and this exception does not change the page-number rule.
+- When the requested value is itself quoted or contains quoted wording or a quoted title, reproduce that quoted portion with ordinary straight double quotation marks (") rather than typographic curly quotes (“ ”), while preserving the exact quoted words, capitalization, spacing, and internal punctuation. Apply this only when quotation marks are part of the requested value or exact span; do not add quotation marks to unquoted answers, do not convert apostrophes, and do not otherwise normalize names, numbers, units, percent signs, parenthetical qualifiers, or full-sentence answers. Existing successful direct extractions remain unchanged, and if the question explicitly requires a different typographic form, follow that explicit requirement.
 - Prefer direct extraction over paraphrase.
-- Return one selected exact numeric or named span as the final answer, not multiple encodings of the same value.
-- When the question already names the field or attribute it asks for (for example, a quantity of a named substance, an arrival or departure time, a designation, a nationality, or a state), return only that entry's value, ending at the value's edge. Do not repeat the field name, row label, column header, or the question's quoted substance, product, or attribute wording that located the entry, even if printed beside the value; treat such appended label text as extra answer content. Preserve the full requested value when its own components carry meaning: keep multiword names, name suffixes, printed parenthetical abbreviations, and other printed components belonging to the selected name; keep the printed unit or representation attached to the selected value; and keep quotation marks or punctuation only when they delimit the requested answer itself (for example, a quoted tagline). Apply these boundaries and the existing exact-copy, spacing, punctuation, unit, and no-alternate-encoding rules to the retained value only. Where existing guidance does not specify whether adjacent printed label text belongs to the answer, this scope rule governs.
-- For questions asking which brand, company, service, or product is advertised or named, identify the distinctive name that answers the question. If the selected span also contains the question's own generic category wording plus a merely descriptive corporate or geographic extension, return the distinctive name and omit only that category wording and descriptive extension, unless the question asks for the full legal or company name or the extension is needed to distinguish the entity. Do not truncate a multiword proper name merely because it is long; keep titles, initials, suffixes, parenthetical abbreviations, and other printed components that identify the requested person or organization.
-- When a requested amount or name appears in more than one form, such as a symbol, written-out words, a currency conversion, or a parenthetical abbreviation, identify the field, cell, or entry that answers the question and return only the value exactly as printed there.
-- For a numeric answer, determine the exact printed value in the selected cell or entry before deciding whether to include a unit symbol; a percent sign, currency symbol, or unit shown only in the question wording or a column heading is contextual and is not appended to a cell value that prints only the number.
-- For a question that supplies the currency or unit in its wording (for example, 'amount in $' or 'price in dollars'), treat that supplied unit as question context and return the numeric value from the selected cell or entry without repeating the question's unit. Under this supplied-unit condition, if the selected entry prints a currency or unit symbol, include it only when the question asks for the amount as printed or when the symbol is needed to distinguish the displayed currency; otherwise return the bare printed number. This supplied-unit rule takes precedence over the general instruction to keep a printed currency symbol in that situation; for questions that do not supply the unit, keep a printed symbol exactly as before. Do not strip a symbol or code that is part of a nonnumeric name or identifier.
-- If the selected value itself prints a percent sign, currency symbol, comma grouping, decimal fraction, or other unit or representation, keep that printed form exactly and do not strip or normalize it merely because another form appears elsewhere, except that a printed currency or unit symbol is handled by the supplied-unit rule above when the question supplies that currency or unit.
-- Do not append restatements, conversions, or alternate forms from elsewhere unless the question explicitly asks for them. If the question asks for a specific representation or unit, use that representation from the document.
-- Keep a parenthetical abbreviation only when it is printed as part of the selected name. Copy the chosen span's internal spacing and punctuation exactly as printed, including whether a space appears before a parenthetical abbreviation; do not insert or remove a space to match conventional prose.
-- When the requested answer is itself a quoted title, tagline, or quoted expression, or contains a quoted word as part of a title, keep the quotation marks but transcribe opening and closing marks as plain ASCII double quotes (") rather than typographic curly quotes (“ ”), unless the question explicitly asks to reproduce the document's typographic punctuation or the quoted text is a literal code/markup string. For these delimiting quotation marks only, this transcription convention governs over the general instruction to copy punctuation exactly as printed; the general exact-copy preference continues to apply to the internal punctuation, spacing, and words inside the quotation marks.
-- Preserve the quoted wording exactly, including capitalization, internal spacing, and all non-quote punctuation; do not strip the quotation marks, add quotation marks where they are not part of the requested answer, or split a coordinated pair of quoted expressions requested by a plural question.
-- Limit this quote-mark convention to quotation marks that delimit requested answer text; keep apostrophes, parenthetical abbreviations, percent signs, currency symbols, commas, decimals, and other printed punctuation unchanged, and retain the complete requested span when it is a full sentence or title.
-- When multiple printed encodings exist, choose the answering entry's exact printed value as the selected span rather than treating every alternate encoding as a separate candidate.
-- After selecting the answer, perform a final character-by-character verification for short spans: re-read the selected span in the document and preserve exactly whether an apostrophe, hyphen, slash, or period is attached to adjacent characters or separated by a space; for short numeric identifiers, check each digit against the printed sequence and use the clearest reading of the requested field rather than substituting a visually similar digit that is merely plausible from context. Keep this verification after the requested entity or field and answer scope are selected; it must not change the selected entry, add alternative encodings or guessed digits, or strip or add units, currency symbols, comma grouping, decimals, percent signs, quoted wording, or multiword name components that belong to the selected value.
+- For questions asking for a business segment, course, event, title, designation, product category, or similar printed named entry, return the exact printed span of the selected entry. Preserve its internal wording and grammatical number exactly, without pluralizing or singularizing it to fit the question. If the same entry or cell visibly includes a trailing schedule/day notation attached to the name, keep that notation as part of the entry when the question asks for that listed entry; do not drop an attached same-entry suffix. Conversely, do not merge a separate schedule, day, time, row/column label, or adjacent field that the document presents apart from the requested name, and do not add such a field merely because it is related or because the question mentions a schedule. If the question asks only for the name and the schedule is separately presented, return the name alone; if it asks for the full listed entry and the printed entry includes the notation, return the full printed entry. This applies to named entries, not to the object noun of a measurement question, and it does not authorize adding labels or narration. Existing correct full-title and designation extractions remain unchanged.
 - Before finalizing, compare the answer against nearby alternatives and keep the best-supported exact span.
